{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "registeredAt": "2026-08-31",
  "purpose": "Pre-registered attribution of every v2 case assertion, so that after the efficacy runs the skill arm can be scored separately on assertions that require following the skill and on assertions a competent model passes without it. Written before those runs exist. It changes no threshold and promotes nothing; eval-report.mjs and eval-verify.mjs do not read it.",
  "whyItExists": "One author wrote the skills, the fixtures, the graders and the reference answers, and derived each rubric from the skill own rules. Part of what a whole-case average measures is therefore vocabulary transfer from injected text to output. Splitting the rubric makes that visible: a real effect should move rule-targeted assertions and leave base-capability ones flat. Movement on both is the signature of a flattering rubric.",
  "classes": {
    "rule-targeted": "Passing requires following something the skill teaches. This is where a skill effect should appear.",
    "base-capability": "A competent model passes without the skill; the assertion exists to stop a fix that breaks the product or deletes the work. No skill credit. Movement here is noise or regression, never evidence of efficacy.",
    "harm-guard": "Detects a plausible harm the skill itself could cause: over-reporting findings that are not there, inventing artifacts, widening scope. The skill arm scoring WORSE here is a cost of the skill and must be reported as one.",
    "integrity-guard": "Confirms the work product was not damaged — inputs left byte-identical, a workflow still present, the app still building. Real and worth checking, but its SUBJECT is mechanically fixed: a reviewer told not to edit does not edit, so it passes in every arm of every case. Membership is decided by that subject, never by an observed pass rate — a harm guard that simply did not trip in the runs so far is a finding about the skill and stays where it is. Kept OUT of harm-guard so it cannot dilute that measurement, and out of base-capability because a contrast arm that cannot vary is not a contrast."
  },
  "preRegistrationCaveat": "26 of the 53 cases had no run bundle when this file was written and are genuine pre-registration. The other 27 already had runs, so their attribution is post-hoc and is marked per case. Post-hoc entries are still useful and are weaker evidence; any analysis that leans on them should say so.",
  "method": "Classified from the assertion id and the skill rule it maps to, with an explicit override list for the cases the pattern got wrong, and never from any recorded result. The override lists are in the commit that added this file.",
